Real-world uses
- Shopping lists and to-do lists: push to add, indexOf + splice to tick something off.
- Queues: customers at a bank, songs "up next" in a music app, print jobs waiting for a printer. New things push onto the back, and the next thing to handle is shifted off the front. Programmers call this first in, first out.
- Undo history: every change is pushed, and the undo button pops the most recent. Your browser's back button works the same way. This is called last in, first out, a stack.
- "Recently viewed" lists in online shops: add to the end, and drop from the start when it gets too long, like your exercise.
- Guest lists and banned words: includes to answer "is this allowed?".

